{"parentUuid":"f45a02b1-fe37-44c5-bdc8-64d500eb9f4d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"af8d90b8a1ac4b5a4","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZQ4PNfVr3G7RVw3htKX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013hdW8dPDhXQz2UoB464Skp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/round-4; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json,re\nd=json.load(open('workflow/round4-result.json'))['findings']\nkeys={'S01':r'C-PRESENT','S02':r'PT\\.realtime|path.trac','S03':r'cpu-topology|topology|NUMA','S04':r'FLUID\\.gpu|GPU fluid','S05':r'TRANS\\.crypto','S06':r'COLLAB\\.binaries|binar','S07':r'rhi-vulkan|macos','S08':r'asymptotic','S09':r'rollback','S10':r'C-RHI|work-graph|state-object','S11':r'A11Y\\.motion|photosens|motion reduc','S12':r'sim-schedule','S13':r'AUD\\.CONTENT\\.dialogue|ducking','S14':r'reference-validation','S15':r'chat-text'}\nfor s,k in keys.items():\n  print('=====',s)\n  for f in d:\n    t=f['target']+' '+f['finding']+' '+str(f.get('proposed_change'))\n    if re.search(k,f['target']+' '+f['finding'],re.I): print(f['id'],'|',f['target'][:80],'|',f['finding'][:300].replace('\\n',' '))\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1192,"cache_read_input_tokens":46120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1192,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013hdW8dPDhXQz2UoB464Skp":{"command":"cd /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/round-4; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json,re\nd=json.load(open('workflow/round4-result.json'))['findings']\nkeys={'S01':r'C-PRESENT','S02':r'PT\\.realtime|path.trac','S03':r'cpu-topology|topology|NUMA','S04':r'FLUID\\.gpu|GPU fluid','S05':r'TRANS\\.crypto','S06':r'COLLAB\\.binaries|binar','S07':r'rhi-vulkan|macos','S08':r'asymptotic','S09':r'rollback','S10':r'C-RHI|work-graph|state-object','S11':r'A11Y\\.motion|photosens|motion reduc','S12':r'sim-schedule','S13':r'AUD\\.CONTENT\\.dialogue|ducking','S14':r'reference-validation','S15':r'chat-text'}\nfor s,k in keys.items():\n  print('=====',s)\n  for f in d:\n    t=f['target']+' '+f['finding']+' '+str(f.get('proposed_change'))\n    if re.search(k,f['target']+' '+f['finding'],re.I): print(f['id'],'|',f['target'][:80],'|',f['finding'][:300].replace('\\n',' '))\nE"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfZQ4NqAw3DpCZLYu2Xph","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4d307229-c495-4672-b8c5-b97eb1cffbe2","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:36:52.455Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
{"parentUuid":"4d307229-c495-4672-b8c5-b97eb1cffbe2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"832e1406-bbbf-40c1-a1f3-aab8fe479c03","agentId":"af8d90b8a1ac4b5a4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013hdW8dPDhXQz2UoB464Skp","type":"tool_result","content":"===== S01\nK-FUTURE-3 | PLAT.PAL.cloud-streaming; PLAT.PAL.cloud-hybrid; skills.json target/platform axe | Streaming-first delivery is modelled only for a build being streamed (session detection, touch overlay, UI scale). The engine-as-cloud-render-host case (headless GPU rendering, frame capture, low-latency hardware encode, video/input back-channel, multi-tenant GPU packing) has no owner or radar entry\n===== S02\nK-RENDER-1 | RND.PT.realtime, RND.GI.restir, RND.GI.rt-required, radar.json | RND.PT.realtime (real-time path tracing, ReSTIR PT class) is marked E while RND.GI.restir (ReSTIR GI & path-traced GI) is M and on the radar. The same technique has two maturity classes. Because E entries need no radar row, real-time PT has no revisit trigger and no fallback, and RND.GI.rt-required \nK-PLATFORM-9 | platform-server-host, PLAT.SRV.pal, rhi-vulkan, render-validation | platform-server-host is defined as 'no display or GPU'. Headless GPU hosts (Linux GPU nodes without a display, running offscreen render, GPU bake or texture encode, GPU cook, path-traced reference validation and cloud rendering) have no platform description, and rhi-vulkan does not declare server-ho\nK-FUTURE-6 | L73; RND.RECON.upscalers; RND.RECON.ml-denoise; profile hwrt | L73 calls upscaling and frame generation 'an optional presentation layer'. True for frame generation, not for upscaling: real-time path-traced and console 4K tiers are budgeted at internal resolution with reconstruction in the pipeline, and hwrt validates no raster fallback. A hwrt budget could omit\n===== S03\n===== S04\nK-SIM-9 | PHY.FLUID.gpu (E), PHY.FLUID.particles (M), PHY.FLUID.grid (M), radar 'Particle  | GPU fluid solvers are E while the particle and grid fluids they run are M. The radar entry is titled 'Particle & GPU fluids' but lists only particles and grid, so PHY.FLUID.gpu has no fallback or revisit trigger.\nK-SIM-14 | RND.GRAPH.external-work, PHY.ARCH.gpu, PHY.FLUID.gpu, PHY.SOFT.cloth, RND.VFX.gp | The external-work arbiter registers streaming, decompression, scene deltas, AS builds and VT pages, but not GPU simulation producers (fluids, cloth, physics offload, crowd VAT). No rule states when gameplay-relevant GPU simulation results are read back or how async readback stays deterministic. Flui\n===== S05\nK-NET-4 | NET.TRANS.crypto; NET.SESS.auth; NET.SESS.handshake; NET.ARCH.versioning; auth-t | Auth-ticket and service-identity validation is stated in two capabilities with different owners (NET.TRANS.crypto and NET.SESS.auth), and the handshake in three (NET.ARCH.versioning, NET.SESS.handshake, the transport crypto handshake). Nothing says where the transport handshake ends and the session \n===== S06\nK-TOOLS-11 | world-editor-viewport (ED.WORLD.blockout, ED.WORLD.mesh-paint), ED.COLLAB.binari | world-editor-viewport spans three literatures (3D manipulation UX, large-world editing, mesh editing) and provides C-EDVIEW on which ~25 domain tools depend, so modeling/paint work shares the contract's change queue. Separately, ED.COLLAB.binaries (large binary storage) sits in ci-cd-automation whil\n===== S07\nK-PLATFORM-9 | platform-server-host, PLAT.SRV.pal, rhi-vulkan, render-validation | platform-server-host is defined as 'no display or GPU'. Headless GPU hosts (Linux GPU nodes without a display, running offscreen render, GPU bake or texture encode, GPU cook, path-traced reference validation and cloud rendering) have no platform description, and rhi-vulkan does not declare server-ho\n===== S08\nK-PERF-3 | PRF.METH.asymptotics, PRF.BENCH.scale-content, milestones M1/M5/M6, C-FRAME, C-F | Structural contracts that fix world-scale asymptotics are frozen at M1 on 2D indie games, but asymptotic declarations and stress worlds are first gated at M5/M6. Their validators are circular: M5 gates scale-content validated by asymptotics, and M6 gates asymptotics validated by scale-content. A sca\nK-TEST-2 | data/milestones.json gates (QA.AGENT.holdout, QA.AGENT.mutation-gate, QA.AGENT.g | Gate validators are not independent and often do not exercise the property validated. About 28 gates have owner and validator in the same workstream (security-engineering and privacy-data-protection sit in quality). Circular pairs: gate-canaries vs agent-boundary; QA.FUNC.soak vs PRF.MEM.footprint; \n===== S09\nK-ARCH-7 | milestones M1-M3 contracts_frozen; indie-2d-client, minimal-client, fighting-2d- | M2 claims indie-2d/minimal/fighting-2d shipping on PC, console, mobile and web while the non-optional contracts C-RG, C-SHADER, C-MATIF, C-RSCENE, C-SCENETEX, C-DRAW2D, C-COLOR, C-TEMPORAL, C-VT, C-PHYS are frozen only at M3; shipped games sit on contracts M3 may change. check.py verifies freeze-bef\nK-ARCH-8 | NET.PRED.lagcomp, PHY.ARCH.rewind, ANM.RT.pose-history, GAM.SYS.hit-detection, G | Lag compensation/server rewind is split across four skills (collider history in physics, pose history in animation, window and protocol in prediction-rollback, hurtbox resolution and rewind validation in gameplay toolkit) with no contract naming the composite 'hit volumes with poses as of tick T'; c\nK-NET-2 | C-NETSESSION; net-session; replication; NET.SESS.baseline/reconnect/travel/join | Session events (join-ready, baseline start, travel, reconnect with ownership restore, disconnect) have no declared direction into replication. replication does not consume C-NETSESSION. net-session consumes C-REP? optionally and not C-SNAPSHOT although baseline is 'replicated snapshot or C-SNAPSHOT \nK-NET-3 | anti-cheat-integrity; net-session; C-INTEGRITY; C-HOSTAUTH; NET.SESS.disconnect | NET.SESS.disconnect owns the ban hook and C-HOSTAUTH claims kick/ban in every host mode, but nothing declares the edge from an integrity verdict to enforcement: anti-cheat-integrity does not consume C-HOSTAUTH and net-session does not consume C-INTEGRITY. Only dedicated-server consumes both, so list\nK-NET-6 | configurations; dedicated-server profiles; NET.SESS.host-mode; NET.PRED.spectato | No configuration has a server or headless target for lockstep or rollback; dedicated-server, replication and server-scaleout carry only the 'online' profile. rts-*, fighting-2d-rollback-* and lockstep tools are client/tools only. Relay-hosted or referee hosting, server-side desync arbitration, serve\nK-NET-7 | NET.PRED.lagcomp; NET.REP.interpolation; C-INPUT; C-PREDICT; C-REP; PHY.ARCH.rew | Lag compensation needs the client view time (interpolation tick pair and delay) at fire time. Replication produces it, C-INPUT frames must carry it, prediction-rollback consumes it, C-PHYS collider history and C-ANIM pose history are queried with it. No contract carries it: C-INPUT is only tick-stam\nK-NET-8 | NET.SESS.host-mode; NET.ARCH.distributed-authority; NET.ARCH.validation | Host migration is a clause inside NET.SESS.host-mode with contributors network-architect and dedicated-server only. It needs replicated state or snapshot, prediction state and authority transfer (replication, determinism-replay, prediction-rollback, distributed-authority) and has no oracle scenario.\nK-NET-11 | ANM.RT.pose-history; NET.PRED.lagcomp; indie-2d-online-moddable-server | ANM.RT.pose-history is restricted to lite3d/std3d, but min2d online configurations contain prediction-rollback with lag compensation; animated 2D hitboxes (skeletal 2D, frame-data sprites) have no history owner.\nK-SIM-1 | PHY.2D.*, physics-2d, collision-detection, rigid-body-dynamics, PHY.COL.queries/ | In every min2d configuration, queries, filtering/layers, CCD, joints, sleeping, kinematic bodies and platform riding are absent from the closure, because collision-detection and rigid-body-dynamics are lite3d/std3d only. physics-2d owns only dynamics, controllers, determinism, shape-gen and cellular\nK-SIM-3 | NET.PRED.physics (E), PHY.ARCH.rewind (E), C-PHYS conformance clause | Networked physics is one E capability. Predicted general rigid-body rollback with partial island resimulation is not established: PhysX and Jolt do not restore solver warm-start caches or partial islands, Chaos network-physics prediction is beta/experimental, and Rocket League is a bespoke Bullet fo\nK-SIM-13 | data/legacy-patterns.json (only L30 and L36 touch physics and simulation) | Missing legacy patterns: discrete-only collision patched with speed caps and thick walls; an always-awake single global physics world with no sleeping, active set or simulation LOD; rollback or lockstep over a backend that cannot restore its solver caches; synchronous per-caller scene queries from A\nK-TOOLS-5 | minimal-tools, indie-2d-tools, fighting-2d-rollback-tools; world-editor-viewport | No tools configuration exists without the editor. minimal-tools (no world) still includes world-editor-viewport, graph-editor-framework and collaboration-version-control because they are profile 'all'; there is no code-first/CLI-only configuration (cook, build, validate via commandlets, text assets)\nK-PERF-1 | PRF.CPU.compiler, BLD.SYS.toolchains, BLD.SYS.configs, XC.DET.float, XC.DET.conf | Three skills claim compiler flags. PRF.CPU.compiler is 'optimization flag policy (sole owner)' but sits in a process skill that owns no build files. BLD.SYS owns the toolchain matrix and configs, and XC.DET.float owns flags and FMA. PGO/LTO/fast-math change FP results, so a performance flag policy c\nK-PERF-2 | CORE.SCALE.actuators, C-SCALE, C-GOVERN, XC.DET.float, NET.SESS.overload, C-SIGN | The governor drives actuators (significance, animation rate, worker count, time dilation) from local, non-deterministic signals. Nothing classifies which actuators may change simulation-visible state. Under lockstep, rollback, server-authoritative play and replay, a client-local governor that alters\nK-PERF-7 | milestones M2/M3/M4/M5, PRF.METH.energy, PRF.NET.resim-cost, PRF.MEM.size, PRF.N | Milestone exits and gates are inconsistent with claimed obligations. M4 exit says server density is within budget, but PRF.NET.server-density is gated only at M6. Rollback at M2 has no PRF.NET.resim-cost gate. lite-3d mobile (M3) and xr-standalone (M5) have no energy or sustained-thermal gate. PRF.M\nK-COMPLETE-1 | GAM.SYS, CORE.FRAME.time, C-SAVE, C-DET | No capability owns game-time timers / latent actions (delays, cooldowns, wait-N-seconds in level scripts, time-domain aware, pausable, saveable, replicated, deterministic). 'timer' appears only as hardware timer precision in PLAT.PAL.clocks. Systems will hand-roll wall-clock timers that break replay\nK-COMPLETE-2 | CORE.SCALE.autodetect, GAM.SAVE.settings, OBS.CRASH | No startup crash-loop detection / safe-mode boot with automatic rollback of settings/mods to last-known-good when a config crashes before UI is reachable.\nK-LEGACY-4 | L27, ARCH.GOV.adr | L27 packs two new dogmas (ECS everywhere, GPU-driven everywhere) into one pattern anchored only to ARCH.GOV.adr, so it cannot be checked against CORE.OBJ.hybrid or RND.ARCH.submission-strategy. Other new dogmas are unrecorded: async-by-default physics (PHY.ARCH.async is E although rollback needs syn\nK-GAMEPLAY-4 | CORE.FRAME.time; GAM.SYS.impacts; GAM.SYS.abilities; C-ABILITY; animation-runtim | Two feedback concerns are unowned. (1) Local time scale: CORE.FRAME.time covers only global real/game/dilation/pause, with no per-entity or per-team scale (hit-stop, bullet-time on a subset) obeyed consistently by animation, physics, VFX, audio, abilities and movement, and deterministic as frame dat\nK-TEST-10 | QA.SIM.netsim, NET.TRANS.simulation, QA.FUNC.network | Netcode acceptance runs against a synthetic parametric link conditioner. Nothing validates its realism against measured WAN, Wi-Fi and cellular traces (bursty loss, reordering, handover), so the M2 rollback thresholds (8 frames under 150 ms / 5% loss) may be met only under unrealistically smooth con\n===== S10\nK-FUTURE-1 | C-GPUTIER; skills.json profile hwrt; RND.GI.rt-required; RND.RHI.caps | C-GPUTIER models hardware as a linear ladder of named tiers ('rt', 'rt+tensor') and features declare a single minimum tier. RT, tensor/matrix, mesh, work-graph and binding capabilities are orthogonal and ship in different combinations (Metal 4 tensor without RT baseline, matrix units without a vendo\nK-FUTURE-2 | data/radar.json; scripts/check.py radar rules; ARCH.GOV.radar | Radar evidence and triggers are time-relative prose ('preview', 'beta', 'no shipped titles') with no as-of date, cited sources or staleness check, so maturity labels cannot be shown honest over a multi-year program and cannot be caught when stale. Labels such as cooperative-vector preview and work-g\n===== S11\nK-GAMEPLAY-2 | accessibility; UI.A11Y.*; QA.CERT.photosensitivity; post-color-hdr; vfx-particle | accessibility's purpose claims 'motion and photosensitivity options', but no capability implements them. The only photosensitivity row is QA.CERT.photosensitivity (late cert testing). There is no runtime flash/luminance/red-flash limiter or reduced-flash mode, no authoring-time flash analysis of VFX\n===== S12\nK-ARCH-10 | CORE.FRAME.sim-schedule, CORE.FRAME.access-model, C-FRAME, docs/00 section 6 | Same owner says both that ordering is derived from declared access by one scheduler and that the simulation schedule is a fixed sequence with declared access used only for validation; agents cannot tell whether adding a system may reorder simulation.\nK-SIM-8 | CORE.FRAME.sim-schedule, CORE.FRAME.phases, CORE.FRAME.access-model, docs/00 lin | sim-schedule says 'fixed sequence; declared access used only for validation', while phases, access-model and the principles say ordering is derived from declared access. The two regimes are not reconciled. Contributors omit ik-procedural-animation, cloth-deformables, vehicle-physics, destruction-fra\nK-LEGACY-1 | CORE.FRAME.sim-schedule, ANM.ARCH.sync, C-FRAME, L01, docs/00 section 6 | CORE.FRAME.sim-schedule says 'fixed sequence per tick; declared access used only for validation'. That is the central sequential update loop (L01) applied to the whole simulation. It contradicts CORE.FRAME.access-model and phases (ordering derived from declared access) and docs/00 section 6 ('canoni\n===== S13\nK-GAMEPLAY-1 | AUD.CONTENT.dialogue; narrative-dialogue; audio-content-runtime; facial-animatio | AUD.CONTENT.dialogue (VO playback and ducking for C-DIALOGUE lines) is owned by narrative-dialogue. narrative-dialogue lists 'VO playback -> audio-content-runtime' as a non-responsibility and facial-animation says 'Dialogue playback -> audio-content-runtime'. audio-content-runtime's purpose includes\n===== S14\n===== S15","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"41a621be-5e6d-4a11-be80-8399879a27ca","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:36:52.559Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4d307229-c495-4672-b8c5-b97eb1cffbe2","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
