8':r'asymptot|replicated entit|connection','S09':r'BLD\\.REL|rollback|release-criteria|soak','S10':r'state-object|work.graph|pipeline.state|C-RHI','S11':r'A11Y|motion|photosens','S14':r'reference|compat|oracle|path.trac|QA\\.RENDER','S15':r'chat|text-fonts|validating|moderation'}\nfor s,k in keys.items():\n  print('=====',s)\n  for f in d:\n    if re.search(k,f['target']+' '+f['finding'],re.I): print(f['id'],'|',f['target'][:70],'|',f['finding'][:200].replace('\\n',' '))\nE"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfZQ4fJsBPoVxjaFrPU7e","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d9043c1c-044d-49af-be42-a54f1f091b7f","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:36:57.760Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
{"parentUuid":"d9043c1c-044d-49af-be42-a54f1f091b7f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"832e1406-bbbf-40c1-a1f3-aab8fe479c03","agentId":"af8d90b8a1ac4b5a4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BoSgWrbiWKmUbD4SKR678V","type":"tool_result","content":"===== S01\nK-FUTURE-3 | PLAT.PAL.cloud-streaming; PLAT.PAL.cloud-hybrid; skills.json target/pl | Streaming-first delivery is modelled only for a build being streamed (session detection, touch overlay, UI scale). The engine-as-cloud-render-host case (headless GPU rendering, frame capture, low-late\nK-FUTURE-6 | L73; RND.RECON.upscalers; RND.RECON.ml-denoise; profile hwrt | L73 calls upscaling and frame generation 'an optional presentation layer'. True for frame generation, not for upscaling: real-time path-traced and console 4K tiers are budgeted at internal resolution \nK-FUTURE-9 | docs/06 A11; B29; CORE.LIFE.language; CORE.LIFE.interop; radar memory- | A11 limits memory-safe languages to parsers of untrusted data while B29/CORE.LIFE.language allow a mixed-language engine, and the radar trigger is only console toolchain support. None records that the\n===== S03\nK-SYSTEMS-1 | C-TASK, CORE.JOBS.*, job-system-task-graph, RES.MGMT.pipeline, ED.ARCH | No capability or C-TASK obligation covers task cancellation (tokens, propagation to dependents, cancelling in-flight IO/GPU work, cancel-vs-completion and cancel-vs-retire ordering). Cancellation appe\nK-SYSTEMS-2 | C-IO, RES.IO.*, C-PAL, PLAT.PAL.os, GAM.SAVE.atomic | The IO stack is read-only. No owner exists for durable write primitives (write scheduling/coalescing, fsync/F_FULLFSYNC, directory sync, atomic replace, disk-full, write-vs-read deadline arbitration).\nK-SYSTEMS-3 | CORE.MEM.allocators, C-MEM, C-LIFETIME, memory-allocators | The allocator family lists 'linear/frame' allocators, but the framework has overlapping frames in flight, multi-frame tasks and per-world cadences, so 'frame' is not a lifetime. memory-allocators cons\nK-SYSTEMS-5 | CORE.CONC.sync, CORE.JOBS.thread-model, CORE.JOBS.priorities, AUD.ARCH | Priority inversion is unaddressed: CORE.CONC.sync specifies futex locks and spin-with-backoff but no priority inheritance/donation, while the framework mixes real-time audio, latency-critical lanes, Q\nK-SYSTEMS-8 | OBS.CRASH.capture, C-CRASH, C-IPC, PLAT.PAL.signals, CORE.MEM.oom | C-CRASH is layer 1 requiring only C-BASE/C-INSTR, yet C-IPC (layer 2) lists the crash handler as an endpoint. No capability owns crash-time safety: async-signal-safe/SEH-safe code, pre-reserved emerge\nK-PLATFORM-1 | C-TARGETPLAT, platform-architect, platform-desktop, platform-console,  | C-TARGETPLAT is a needs_implementer contract (cook format variants, shader backend hook, packager, deploy/launch/debug), but no capability names its content. Only PLAT.CON.devkit and PLAT.PAL.devlink \nK-PLATFORM-9 | platform-server-host, PLAT.SRV.pal, rhi-vulkan, render-validation | platform-server-host is defined as 'no display or GPU'. Headless GPU hosts (Linux GPU nodes without a display, running offscreen render, GPU bake or texture encode, GPU cook, path-traced reference val\nK-PLATFORM-10 | ML.RT.npu, ML.RT.os-models, C-ML, PLAT.PAL.capability-tiers | ML.RT.npu (NPU backends via platform ML APIs) is marked X although platform NPU paths (Core ML/Neural Engine, Windows ML/DirectML on Copilot+ PCs, Android accelerator APIs) ship, while ML.RT.os-models\nK-TOOLS-9 | editor-ui-framework, editor-architect, C-CFG, config-layers | No capability owns the editor surfaces of configuration and workspace: schema-driven project-settings editor over layered per-platform C-CFG, editor preferences, layout persistence, keybindings, comma\nK-PERF-4 | PRF.CPU.*, PRF.BENCH.lab, PRF.METH.budgets, CORE.JOBS.scheduler, ARCH. | No capability owns parallel scalability: work/span and critical-path analysis of the compiled task graph, scheduling overhead per task and granularity, worker starvation, and core-count scaling curves\nK-PROD-1 | ARCH.PROD (new ARCH.PROD.support-policy), BLD.CI.backports, XC.EXT.ver | No capability owns the engine support commitment: LTS duration, N-1 support, end-of-support dates, platform-SDK/store-mandate sunset and licensee migration deadlines. BLD.CI.backports owns mechanics, \nK-FUTURE-3 | PLAT.PAL.cloud-streaming; PLAT.PAL.cloud-hybrid; skills.json target/pl | Streaming-first delivery is modelled only for a build being streamed (session detection, touch overlay, UI scale). The engine-as-cloud-render-host case (headless GPU rendering, frame capture, low-late\nK-FUTURE-5 | NET.ARCH.meshing; PLAT.PAL.cloud-hybrid; profile persistent-world | Server meshing is M on one live deployment with sparse postmortems, while cloud-hybrid is X on one shipped title; the evidence bars are inconsistent. Meshing is bundled in persistent-world, so a shipp\nK-COMPLETE-1 | GAM.SYS, CORE.FRAME.time, C-SAVE, C-DET | No capability owns game-time timers / latent actions (delays, cooldowns, wait-N-seconds in level scripts, time-domain aware, pausable, saveable, replicated, deterministic). 'timer' appears only as har\nK-LEGACY-4 | L27, ARCH.GOV.adr | L27 packs two new dogmas (ECS everywhere, GPU-driven everywhere) into one pattern anchored only to ARCH.GOV.adr, so it cannot be checked against CORE.OBJ.hybrid or RND.ARCH.submission-strategy. Other \nK-GAMEPLAY-10 | AUD.ARCH.devices; AUD.ARCH.listeners; PLAT.PAL.system-events; INP.DEV. | AUD.ARCH.devices is a single row. Not named: endpoint change and hot-swap (headset, Bluetooth, HDMI) with sample-rate and channel-layout renegotiation; multi-endpoint output (main mix plus per-local-p\n===== S06\nK-SYSTEMS-2 | C-IO, RES.IO.*, C-PAL, PLAT.PAL.os, GAM.SAVE.atomic | The IO stack is read-only. No owner exists for durable write primitives (write scheduling/coalescing, fsync/F_FULLFSYNC, directory sync, atomic replace, disk-full, write-vs-read deadline arbitration).\nK-PLATFORM-6 | PLAT.SVC.*, platform-services, QA.CERT.store-policy, platform_variants | The platform matrix promises 'OS x ISA x API x store', but platform_variants has no store axis. No capability owns PC storefront SDK adapters (Steamworks, Epic Online Services, GOG Galaxy, Microsoft S\nK-TOOLS-11 | world-editor-viewport (ED.WORLD.blockout, ED.WORLD.mesh-paint), ED.COL | world-editor-viewport spans three literatures (3D manipulation UX, large-world editing, mesh editing) and provides C-EDVIEW on which ~25 domain tools depend, so modeling/paint work shares the contract\n===== S07\nK-RENDER-4 | C-SCENETEX, C-MATH, C-TEMPORAL, RND.ARCH.scene-textures | Depth and projection conventions (reverse-Z, float depth, infinite far plane, jitter in clip space, pixel-center and Y-flip differences across D3D/Vulkan/Metal/WebGPU) have no capability. C-SCENETEX l\nK-PLATFORM-1 | C-TARGETPLAT, platform-architect, platform-desktop, platform-console,  | C-TARGETPLAT is a needs_implementer contract (cook format variants, shader backend hook, packager, deploy/launch/debug), but no capability names its content. Only PLAT.CON.devkit and PLAT.PAL.devlink \nK-PLATFORM-9 | platform-server-host, PLAT.SRV.pal, rhi-vulkan, render-validation | platform-server-host is defined as 'no display or GPU'. Headless GPU hosts (Linux GPU nodes without a display, running offscreen render, GPU bake or texture encode, GPU cook, path-traced reference val\nK-FUTURE-1 | C-GPUTIER; skills.json profile hwrt; RND.GI.rt-required; RND.RHI.caps | C-GPUTIER models hardware as a linear ladder of named tiers ('rt', 'rt+tensor') and features declare a single minimum tier. RT, tensor/matrix, mesh, work-graph and binding capabilities are orthogonal \n===== S08\nK-NET-5 | dedicated-server purpose; NET.SESS.budgets; NET.SESS.overload; NET.SRV | dedicated-server's purpose still claims overload degradation and per-connection budgets, which are net-session capabilities (NET.SESS.budgets/overload, C-HOSTAUTH). The admin surface is in both C-HOST\nK-NET-9 | NET.ARCH.budgets; C-BUDGET; PRF.NET.bandwidth; NET.TRANS.simulation | NET.ARCH.budgets (network-architect) and C-BUDGET (performance-architect) both hold budget numbers, but C-BUDGET names no network line (per-connection bytes/s, server egress per CCU, tick cost per pla\nK-PERF-3 | PRF.METH.asymptotics, PRF.BENCH.scale-content, milestones M1/M5/M6, C- | Structural contracts that fix world-scale asymptotics are frozen at M1 on 2D indie games, but asymptotic declarations and stress worlds are first gated at M5/M6. Their validators are circular: M5 gate\nK-LEGACY-3 | legacy-patterns.json (new patterns), CORE.JOBS.thread-model, GAM.SAVE. | Catalogue gaps with only implicit stances. (a) Thread-per-subsystem or per-connection blocking threads: L03 covers only game/render. (b) Wall-clock or sleep-based gameplay timers (L57 covers only test\nK-TEST-2 | data/milestones.json gates (QA.AGENT.holdout, QA.AGENT.mutation-gate,  | Gate validators are not independent and often do not exercise the property validated. About 28 gates have owner and validator in the same workstream (security-engineering and privacy-data-protection s\n===== S09\nK-ARCH-5 | robustness-fuzzing, functional-automation-soak, simulation-validation, | Four process skills author every conformance suite: functional-automation-soak 35 contracts, robustness-fuzzing 26, simulation-validation 26, render-validation 24. robustness-fuzzing (5 capabilities) \nK-ARCH-7 | milestones M1-M3 contracts_frozen; indie-2d-client, minimal-client, fi | M2 claims indie-2d/minimal/fighting-2d shipping on PC, console, mobile and web while the non-optional contracts C-RG, C-SHADER, C-MATIF, C-RSCENE, C-SCENETEX, C-DRAW2D, C-COLOR, C-TEMPORAL, C-VT, C-PH\nK-ARCH-8 | NET.PRED.lagcomp, PHY.ARCH.rewind, ANM.RT.pose-history, GAM.SYS.hit-de | Lag compensation/server rewind is split across four skills (collider history in physics, pose history in animation, window and protocol in prediction-rollback, hurtbox resolution and rewind validation\nK-NET-2 | C-NETSESSION; net-session; replication; NET.SESS.baseline/reconnect/tr | Session events (join-ready, baseline start, travel, reconnect with ownership restore, disconnect) have no declared direction into replication. replication does not consume C-NETSESSION. net-session co\nK-NET-3 | anti-cheat-integrity; net-session; C-INTEGRITY; C-HOSTAUTH; NET.SESS.d | NET.SESS.disconnect owns the ban hook and C-HOSTAUTH claims kick/ban in every host mode, but nothing declares the edge from an integrity verdict to enforcement: anti-cheat-integrity does not consume C\nK-NET-6 | configurations; dedicated-server profiles; NET.SESS.host-mode; NET.PRE | No configuration has a server or headless target for lockstep or rollback; dedicated-server, replication and server-scaleout carry only the 'online' profile. rts-*, fighting-2d-rollback-* and lockstep\nK-NET-7 | NET.PRED.lagcomp; NET.REP.interpolation; C-INPUT; C-PREDICT; C-REP; PH | Lag compensation needs the client view time (interpolation tick pair and delay) at fire time. Replication produces it, C-INPUT frames must carry it, prediction-rollback consumes it, C-PHYS collider hi\nK-NET-8 | NET.SESS.host-mode; NET.ARCH.distributed-authority; NET.ARCH.validatio | Host migration is a clause inside NET.SESS.host-mode with contributors network-architect and dedicated-server only. It needs replicated state or snapshot, prediction state and authority transfer (repl\nK-NET-11 | ANM.RT.pose-history; NET.PRED.lagcomp; indie-2d-online-moddable-server | ANM.RT.pose-history is restricted to lite3d/std3d, but min2d online configurations contain prediction-rollback with lag compensation; animated 2D hitboxes (skeletal 2D, frame-data sprites) have no his\nK-SIM-1 | PHY.2D.*, physics-2d, collision-detection, rigid-body-dynamics, PHY.CO | In every min2d configuration, queries, filtering/layers, CCD, joints, sleeping, kinematic bodies and platform riding are absent from the closure, because collision-detection and rigid-body-dynamics ar\nK-SIM-3 | NET.PRED.physics (E), PHY.ARCH.rewind (E), C-PHYS conformance clause | Networked physics is one E capability. Predicted general rigid-body rollback with partial island resimulation is not established: PhysX and Jolt do not restore solver warm-start caches or partial isla\nK-SIM-13 | data/legacy-patterns.json (only L30 and L36 touch physics and simulati | Missing legacy patterns: discrete-only collision patched with speed caps and thick walls; an always-awake single global physics world with no sleeping, active set or simulation LOD; rollback or lockst\nK-PLATFORM-3 | BLD.REL.packaging, BLD.REL.submission, BLD.REL.patching, BLD.CI.device | Console packaging and signing tools, submission validators, mandated patch/DLC formats and size rules, devkit CI lanes and pre-submission checkers are NDA material. They are owned by non-NDA skills. P\nK-PLATFORM-6 | PLAT.SVC.*, platform-services, QA.CERT.store-policy, platform_variants | The platform matrix promises 'OS x ISA x API x store', but platform_variants has no store axis. No capability owns PC storefront SDK adapters (Steamworks, Epic Online Services, GOG Galaxy, Microsoft S\nK-TOOLS-5 | minimal-tools, indie-2d-tools, fighting-2d-rollback-tools; world-edito | No tools configuration exists without the editor. minimal-tools (no world) still includes world-editor-viewport, graph-editor-framework and collaboration-version-control because they are profile 'all'\nK-PERF-1 | PRF.CPU.compiler, BLD.SYS.toolchains, BLD.SYS.configs, XC.DET.float, X | Three skills claim compiler flags. PRF.CPU.compiler is 'optimization flag policy (sole owner)' but sits in a process skill that owns no build files. BLD.SYS owns the toolchain matrix and configs, and \nK-PERF-2 | CORE.SCALE.actuators, C-SCALE, C-GOVERN, XC.DET.float, NET.SESS.overlo | The governor drives actuators (significance, animation rate, worker count, time dilation) from local, non-deterministic signals. Nothing classifies which actuators may change simulation-visible state.\nK-PERF-7 | milestones M2/M3/M4/M5, PRF.METH.energy, PRF.NET.resim-cost, PRF.MEM.s | Milestone exits and gates are inconsistent with claimed obligations. M4 exit says server density is within budget, but PRF.NET.server-density is gated only at M6. Rollback at M2 has no PRF.NET.resim-c\nK-COMPLETE-1 | GAM.SYS, CORE.FRAME.time, C-SAVE, C-DET | No capability owns game-time timers / latent actions (delays, cooldowns, wait-N-seconds in level scripts, time-domain aware, pausable, saveable, replicated, deterministic). 'timer' appears only as har\nK-COMPLETE-2 | CORE.SCALE.autodetect, GAM.SAVE.settings, OBS.CRASH | No startup crash-loop detection / safe-mode boot with automatic rollback of settings/mods to last-known-good when a config crashes before UI is reachable.\nK-LEGACY-4 | L27, ARCH.GOV.adr | L27 packs two new dogmas (ECS everywhere, GPU-driven everywhere) into one pattern anchored only to ARCH.GOV.adr, so it cannot be checked against CORE.OBJ.hybrid or RND.ARCH.submission-strategy. Other \nK-GAMEPLAY-4 | CORE.FRAME.time; GAM.SYS.impacts; GAM.SYS.abilities; C-ABILITY; animat | Two feedback concerns are unowned. (1) Local time scale: CORE.FRAME.time covers only global real/game/dilation/pause, with no per-entity or per-team scale (hit-stop, bullet-time on a subset) obeyed co\nK-TEST-1 | data/milestones.json (M0-M7 skills/contracts_frozen/gates); test-archi | No milestone lists any process skill in `skills`. M0 freezes C-MEM, C-SYNC, C-TASK, C-MATH and other contracts whose oracle_author is robustness-fuzzing. M0 gates name validators owned by simulation-v\nK-TEST-2 | data/milestones.json gates (QA.AGENT.holdout, QA.AGENT.mutation-gate,  | Gate validators are not independent and often do not exercise the property validated. About 28 gates have owner and validator in the same workstream (security-engineering and privacy-data-protection s\nK-TEST-4 | contracts.json oracle_author assignments; CORE.CONC.correctness; robus | Oracle authorship is assigned by category, not expertise. robustness-fuzzing (libFuzzer, sanitizers, fault injection, TSan) authors 26 semantic contracts needing model checking, litmus, linearizabilit\nK-TEST-10 | QA.SIM.netsim, NET.TRANS.simulation, QA.FUNC.network | Netcode acceptance runs against a synthetic parametric link conditioner. Nothing validates its realism against measured WAN, Wi-Fi and cellular traces (bursty loss, reordering, handover), so the M2 ro\nK-SEC-2 | untrusted-inputs.json; NET.TRANS.local-network; NET.SRV.community-host | Parsers of hostile bytes have no registry entry, so the fuzz and limits proof never covers them. Missing inputs: LAN discovery broadcast/mDNS replies, server-browser listings and query responses, comm\n===== S10\nK-FUTURE-1 | C-GPUTIER; skills.json profile hwrt; RND.GI.rt-required; RND.RHI.caps | C-GPUTIER models hardware as a linear ladder of named tiers ('rt', 'rt+tensor') and features declare a single minimum tier. RT, tensor/matrix, mesh, work-graph and binding capabilities are orthogonal \nK-FUTURE-2 | data/radar.json; scripts/check.py radar rules; ARCH.GOV.radar | Radar evidence and triggers are time-relative prose ('preview', 'beta', 'no shipped titles') with no as-of date, cited sources or staleness check, so maturity labels cannot be shown honest over a mult\nK-FUTURE-4 | RND.GEO.splats; CNT.IMP.splats; RND.GEO.splat-relight; radar splat ent | Three splat entries share identical evidence but carry different classes: static rendering is M while its required import is X, so a consumer sits in a higher class than its input path. M rests on res\n===== S11\nK-ARCH-2 | skills.json profiles/targets, configurations minimal-client and online | Scale-down is proven only as closure, not minimality. Membership is profile 'all' or match, and most skills are tagged all, so minimal-client (no physics UI/card game) has 74 skills vs 98 for standard\nK-ARCH-12 | engine-architect, ARCH.ORG.escalation, C-A11Y | engine-architect has 29 direct children and is sole arbiter for intra-workstream, cross-workstream and party-lead disputes with no workstream coordinator; also C-A11Y is the only contract never frozen\nK-PLATFORM-8 | UI.A11Y.screen-reader, UI.TXT.ime, ARCH.STRUCT.platform-backends, plat | The backend-slot registry names an IME/a11y bridge, but only console has a named owner. For desktop, mobile and web the OS accessibility bridges (UIA, NSAccessibility, AT-SPI, UIAccessibility/TalkBack\nK-TOOLS-1 | C-EDHOST, C-EDVIEW, ED.WORLD.preview-scenes, ED.UI.asset-editor-host;  | Preview-world creation is exposed via C-EDVIEW, but asset editors are told to embed preview worlds through C-EDHOST, which requires only C-EDCMD. editor-ui-framework cannot consume C-EDVIEW (world-edi\nK-GAMEPLAY-2 | accessibility; UI.A11Y.*; QA.CERT.photosensitivity; post-color-hdr; vf | accessibility's purpose claims 'motion and photosensitivity options', but no capability implements them. The only photosensitivity row is QA.CERT.photosensitivity (late cert testing). There is no runt\nK-GAMEPLAY-3 | narrative-dialogue; gameplay-data; cinematics-sequencer; gameplay-came | ANM, AUD, PHY, AI/NAV, UI and A11Y each have a validation capability. The content-heavy domains have none: dialogue/narrative graphs (unreachable or dead-end nodes, soft-locks, fact/flag define-use, p\nK-GAMEPLAY-5 | C-ANIM; ANM.RT.blending; ANM.GRAPH.graph; ANM.TOOL.asset-editor; GAM.S | C-ANIM promises 'action/montage requests' and ANM.TOOL.asset-editor authors montages, but no runtime capability owns montages: gameplay-driven one-shots, slots layered into graphs, sections and jumps,\nK-GAMEPLAY-9 | gameplay-systems-toolkit; GAM.SYS.volumes; GAM.AI.smart-objects; INP.A | No interaction and target-selection system exists: interactable discovery and focus, hold-to-interact, contextual prompts with the active device glyph and localized text, soft-target and lock-on selec\nK-GAMEPLAY-14 | UI.A11Y.assists; GAM.DATA.tuning; GAM.SYS.aim-assist; XC.SEC.score-int | A11Y.assists (assist and difficulty hooks) and DATA.tuning (difficulty variants) share territory with no mutual contributor entry. Nothing defines how per-player assists (extended timers, auto-aim, in\n===== S14\nK-RENDER-1 | RND.PT.realtime, RND.GI.restir, RND.GI.rt-required, radar.json | RND.PT.realtime (real-time path tracing, ReSTIR PT class) is marked E while RND.GI.restir (ReSTIR GI & path-traced GI) is M and on the radar. The same technique has two maturity classes. Because E ent\nK-RENDER-6 | RND.ARCH.multiview, RND.ARCH.editor-rendering, ui-architect, GAM.CAM.p | Runtime offscreen render products are not owned: isolated render scenes with their own lighting and budget for 3D UI (character creator, inventory previews, portraits, icons), in-world render-to-textu\nK-ARCH-2 | skills.json profiles/targets, configurations minimal-client and online | Scale-down is proven only as closure, not minimality. Membership is profile 'all' or match, and most skills are tagged all, so minimal-client (no physics UI/card game) has 74 skills vs 98 for standard\nK-ARCH-4 | PHY.ARCH.validation, ANM.ARCH.validation, AUD.ARCH.validation, GAM.AI. | Oracle suites are owned twice: physics/animation/audio 'oracle suite' capabilities belong to the implementing leads while QA.SIM.* own the same metrics and simulation-validation is the declared oracle\nK-ARCH-5 | robustness-fuzzing, functional-automation-soak, simulation-validation, | Four process skills author every conformance suite: functional-automation-soak 35 contracts, robustness-fuzzing 26, simulation-validation 26, render-validation 24. robustness-fuzzing (5 capabilities) \nK-ARCH-6 | ARCH.ORG.ownership-ledger, ARCH.ORG.agent-continuity, ARCH.ORG.write-s | The coordination machinery is software (lease/lock server with fencing tokens, per-path access-class enforcement incl. sealed:oracle, work queue, provenance store, fitness-function runner) but is owne\nK-NET-8 | NET.SESS.host-mode; NET.ARCH.distributed-authority; NET.ARCH.validatio | Host migration is a clause inside NET.SESS.host-mode with contributors network-architect and dedicated-server only. It needs replicated state or snapshot, prediction state and authority transfer (repl\nK-PLATFORM-9 | platform-server-host, PLAT.SRV.pal, rhi-vulkan, render-validation | platform-server-host is defined as 'no display or GPU'. Headless GPU hosts (Linux GPU nodes without a display, running offscreen render, GPU bake or texture encode, GPU cook, path-traced reference val\nK-TOOLS-3 | milestones M0-M3 (contracts_draft/frozen); C-EDCMD, C-EDHOST, C-EDVIEW | Tool contracts are drafted at M2 and frozen at M3, but skills owning the tool halves of domains arrive earlier (M0: material-system, post-color-hdr, render-2d-vector, texture-streaming-vt, input-syste\nK-TOOLS-9 | editor-ui-framework, editor-architect, C-CFG, config-layers | No capability owns the editor surfaces of configuration and workspace: schema-driven project-settings editor over layered per-platform C-CFG, editor preferences, layout persistence, keybindings, comma\nK-PERF-6 | PRF.METH.gates, PRF.BENCH.lab, PRF.BENCH.regression, C-BENCH, BLD.CI.d | All perf gates are wall-clock measurements on exclusive, thermally conditioned reference hardware, including NDA devkits. With many parallel autonomous agents the lab is the scarcest resource. No chea\nK-PERF-8 | C-BUDGET, PRF.METH.budgets, L70 | C-BUDGET's summary lists no percentile frame-time (p99/p99.9), hitches-per-hour, stream-in speed at traversal speed, startup and transition time, or input-to-photon latency lines. Those exist only as \nK-PROD-3 | ARCH.PROD.customer-corpus, ARCH.PROD.intake, QA.REF.dogfood, milestone | Every customer of the engine is agent-authored: reference games are written by the same organization against the public API and dogfood is internal. There is no external design-partner or early-access\nK-PROD-7 | ED.UI.ux, ED.ARCH.discipline-workflows, XC.DX.creator-docs, ARCH.ORG.h | Editor usability and creator documentation are validated only by agent-runnable oracles (iteration targets, doc snippet tests). Creator experience for artists, designers and audio staff cannot be judg\nK-FUTURE-6 | L73; RND.RECON.upscalers; RND.RECON.ml-denoise; profile hwrt | L73 calls upscaling and frame generation 'an optional presentation layer'. True for frame generation, not for upscaling: real-time path-traced and console 4K tiers are budgeted at internal resolution \nK-GAMEPLAY-8 | GAM.SAVE.model; GAM.SAVE.platform; GAM.SAVE.migration; PLAT.SVC.user-m | Persistence rows omit (a) tolerating or preserving unknown content when a mod or DLC that wrote data is absent, (b) a save-slot catalogue with locale-independent metadata (playtime, thumbnail, version\nK-TEST-1 | data/milestones.json (M0-M7 skills/contracts_frozen/gates); test-archi | No milestone lists any process skill in `skills`. M0 freezes C-MEM, C-SYNC, C-TASK, C-MATH and other contracts whose oracle_author is robustness-fuzzing. M0 gates name validators owned by simulation-v\nK-TEST-2 | data/milestones.json gates (QA.AGENT.holdout, QA.AGENT.mutation-gate,  | Gate validators are not independent and often do not exercise the property validated. About 28 gates have owner and validator in the same workstream (security-engineering and privacy-data-protection s\nK-TEST-3 | contracts.json oracle_reference on ~70 layer 3-5 conformance contracts | oracle_reference (external ground truth) is mandatory only for layers 0-2 (check.py line 367). About 70 layer 3-5 conformance contracts name none, exactly the physics, animation, audio, netcode, text,\nK-TEST-4 | contracts.json oracle_author assignments; CORE.CONC.correctness; robus | Oracle authorship is assigned by category, not expertise. robustness-fuzzing (libFuzzer, sanitizers, fault injection, TSan) authors 26 semantic contracts needing model checking, litmus, linearizabilit\nK-TEST-5 | QA.AGENT.holdout, BLD.CI.sealed-suites, QA.AGENT.oracle-independence,  | The sealed holdout has no anti-probing protocol. Results go to the implementer as pass/fail plus failure category with no attempt budget per work package, no retirement or rotation of a failed or quer\nK-TEST-7 | QA.RENDER.*, RND.SHADER.toolchain, RND.SHADER.permutations, C-SHADER,  | Nothing validates the shader toolchain itself: HLSL/Slang to DXIL, SPIR-V, MSL and WGSL translation, cross-backend numeric equivalence, compile-all-permutations gating, miscompile detection, and quali\nK-TEST-8 | C-TESTHOST, C-DET | C-TESTHOST is universal and all evidence flows through it, but its only oracle_reference ships with the runner, so the implementer authors it. The canary known-red case runs through the same runner. C\n===== S15\nK-PLATFORM-8 | UI.A11Y.screen-reader, UI.TXT.ime, ARCH.STRUCT.platform-backends, plat | The backend-slot registry names an IME/a11y bridge, but only console has a named owner. For desktop, mobile and web the OS accessibility bridges (UIA, NSAccessibility, AT-SPI, UIAccessibility/TalkBack\nK-PROD-3 | ARCH.PROD.customer-corpus, ARCH.PROD.intake, QA.REF.dogfood, milestone | Every customer of the engine is agent-authored: reference games are written by the same organization against the public API and dogfood is internal. There is no external design-partner or early-access\nK-GAMEPLAY-12 | legacy-patterns.json (L72 stance_capabilities; new patterns); navigati | The legacy catalogue lacks four recurring stances in these domains: ASCII, bitmap-font or code-unit text without shaping/bidi/grapheme handling; sentence assembly by string concatenation (L72's stance","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"35f2545d-dec0-4450-a1c6-bf167d8ef3b9","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:36:57.881Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d9043c1c-044d-49af-be42-a54f1f091b7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
